concern among firefighters nationwide  about a potential on-the-job hazard that  they say they face  cancer is already the leading cause of  death among firefighters and there's a  fresh theory about why we spoke with  some 44 firefighters who filed lawsuits  this week alleging that the gear they  wear designed to protect them from fires  is also making them sick many in the  firefighting community believe that  toxins are not only present in the fires  themselves the old theory but also in  their so-called turnout gear the  lawsuits claim chemicals used to make  that gear flame water and oil resistant  carry a hidden danger that can cause  devastating illness  this is a current set of turnover that i  would wear if i was working at the  firehouse veteran brockton massachusetts  firefighter joe marchetti wore gear like  this on almost every call for years this  set of gear is actually made so that we  can have a harness inside rising through  the ranks to become deputy chief of his  department to see it say brockton  deputies got to give you a sense of  pride  it took a lot of hard work but now he  won't let this iconic symbol of the job  once worn as almost a second skin  make it past the garage i won't bring it  into my house with what we know about  the turnout gear now and the chemicals  that are in it in 2016 at the age of 46  marchetti was diagnosed with prostate  cancer a diagnosis he alleges was caused  by exposure to chemicals in that gear  chemicals known as pfas per and  polyfluorokill substances last year a  blood test revealed marchetti's pfas  levels were significantly elevated above  the general population these chemicals  have been known to cause harm  for a long time  and this gear was being supplied to us  to protect us pfas are a group of  artificial compounds that make pans  non-stick and materials waterproof for  fire retardant among other things but  new lawsuits also claim they're  associated with adverse health effects  including cancer in the protective gear  worn by firefighters they're called the  forever chemicals and once we  manufacture some of them they last  forever they just migrate through  different systems if they're inhaled or  ingested by humans they'll end up in  your body and they'll stay there for  many years university of notre dame  physics professor graham peasley had  already been studying the presence of  pifas in the environment when the wife  of another firefighter with prostate  cancer convinced him to take a look at  the turnout gear so this is where the  beam particles will come out of the  accelerator at the nuclear research lab  on campus his team analyzed 30 different  sets of protective gear noting an outer  shell a moisture barrier and a thermal  liner we observe the p-fast there at  high quantities and they come off the  outer layer of the skier was really  highly fluorinated and the moisture  barrier was really highly fluorinated  and that's bad that's bad subsequent  studies co-authored by peasley confirm  the presence of phos and other turnout  gear his research has been cited in the  lawsuits  meanwhile the firefighting community is  grappling with staggering rates of  cancer since 2015 nearly three out of  four firefighters added to the iaff  fallen firefighter memorial have died  from so-called occupational cancer  even more have been diagnosed i was kind  of hit like a mack truck mark stelling a  fire captain for the city of gilroy  california found out he had stage one  cancer after an accident caused a tumor  on his kidney to burst when i was laid  out in that hospital bed for eight days  that was  belittling to me because i thought of  myself as pretty tough guy  but  laying there helpless trying to figure  out what was going on  that was a whole another game  retired san jose california fire  investigator teresa malden battled  multiple cancers i had breast cancer in  both breasts i had a double mastectomy  and then the fourth year  the bladder cancer reoccurred so for  four years i battled  and i just  couldn't stay on the job all three of  you are cancer free as we speak  no  and retired san jose fire captain dan  stapp has undergone radiation and  hormone therapy for prostate cancer from  what i understand is it's just going to  be there and just depends how aggressive  we take care of it how aggressive the  cancer itself gets  all three believe they would have been  cancer free if not for their uniforms  they joined a lawsuit in 2020 alleging  that pfos in their gear caused their  illnesses they're seeking damages from  companies that make pfos and manufacture  turnout gear what part of your brain if  any part was considering the risk of the  gear you were wearing  zero  nothing never your gear was almost  attached to your hip their training and  everything else was okay you're gonna go  do dangerous stuff you're to do the best  to protect yourself put this gear on and  go do it it was so ingrained in us it  was a safety thing this was the thing  that i wore it was the thing that i let  my daughter dress up in because she  wanted to look like mom would you  recommend this line of work for your  children and grandchildren  i steered my daughter away from it  you don't want your kids to go through  this  it seems like it was a hard conversation  to  have still is  you know your kids look up to you  they thought i was invincible this is my  son  as for marchetti he married into a  firefighting family so it was only  natural that his son would follow  you are the  wife of a firefighter the daughter of a  firefighter the granddaughter of a  firefighter  and the mother  marchetti's wife jen has always known  that there were risks associated with  the profession it's a dangerous job and  you know that to know that his gear  that's meant to protect him and keep him  from harm  harmed him and changed him  it makes me  it makes me angry it makes me angry and  it could potentially change my other  family members your son my son